{"term": "libertinaggio", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "debauchery", "concept": "debauchery", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 25, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The practice of living without moral or sexual restraint; libertinism or habitual sexual licentiousness as a deliberate lifestyle.", "english_term": "libertinism", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Derives from 'libertino' (libertine); implies systematic philosophical rejection of moral constraints, not merely occasional excess; used in both religious-moral polemics and secular literary contexts from the 17th century onward."}
{"term": "concubina", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "kept_woman", "concept": "concubinage", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 35, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman who cohabits with a man without being his legal wife, holding subordinate social and legal status; a concubine.", "english_term": "concubine", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Recognized from biblical and classical Latin contexts; still used in historical and theological discussions; the institution was legally recognized in pre-unification Italian states and debated in canon law; the male equivalent 'concubino' exists but is rarely attested."}
{"term": "concubinato", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "cohabitation", "concept": "concubinage", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 76, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The institutionalized practice or legal condition of a man living with a concubine; concubinage as a social and legal arrangement distinct from both marriage and prostitution.", "english_term": "concubinage", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More abstract and institutional than 'concubina'; found in historical legal texts, canon law discussions, and civil law treatises; rarely used in everyday modern Italian; the Italian Civil Code of 1942 did not recognize concubinage, pushing the term into historical register."}
{"term": "pederastia", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "ephebophilia", "concept": "ephebophilia", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 45, "technical": 60, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual attraction to or activity with adolescent males; in 19th-century Italian medical-legal usage, also the standard clinical term for male homosexuality in general regardless of partner age.", "english_term": "pederasty", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In 19th-century Italian sexology and criminology (Lombroso, Tamassia), 'pederasta' was the clinical label for a gay man with no age-specific restriction; the term progressively narrowed to the adult-minor sense across the 20th century; in modern Italian 'pederastia' is understood as sexual abuse of boys, distinct from 'omosessualità'; a significant semantic narrowing over 150 years obscures the word's historical breadth."}
{"term": "tribadismo", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "acts", "subcategory": "tribadism", "concept": "tribadism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 80, "recognition_pct_current": 32, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Clinical and archaic term for vulva-to-vulva genital friction between two women; the historical medical term for what is now called tribbing or scissoring.", "english_term": "tribadism", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Used in 19th-century Italian sexological literature; largely replaced in modern slang by 'tribbing' and in everyday speech by 'sesso lesbico'; retained in academic and historical contexts; the practitioner was historically called 'tribade'; derived from Greek 'tribein' (to rub); Lombroso discussed 'tribadismo' as a form of 'inversione sessuale' among women."}
{"term": "inversione sessuale", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "homosexuality", "concept": "homosexual", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 38, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual inversion; the 19th-century Italian sexological term for homosexuality, framing same-sex attraction as a reversal of the normal heterosexual instinct.", "english_term": "sexual inversion", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Andrea Tamassia's 1878 article 'Sull'inversione dell'istinto sessuale' introduced the term to Italian medical literature, paralleling Krafft-Ebing's German framing; the model implied that a homosexual man possessed a female psyche in a male body (congenital neurological anomaly rather than vice); replaced by 'omosessualità' across the 20th century; closely associated with Lombrosian criminal anthropology."}
{"term": "perversione", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "paraphilia", "concept": "paraphilia_general", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 30, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 94, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual perversion; any sexual impulse, desire, or practice deviating from heterosexual procreative norms, framed in moral, religious, or historical clinical terms.", "english_term": "perversion", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Common word in modern Italian but its specifically sexual-pathological register is archaic or literary; 'perversione sessuale' was the standard Italian translation of Krafft-Ebing's clinical categories; replaced in clinical usage by 'parafilia'; in contemporary colloquial Italian 'perversione' often functions as ironic mild praise ('che perversione!' = 'how kinky!'), marking a register shift from condemnation to humor."}
{"term": "piaceri venerici", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "sensuality", "concept": "carnal_desire", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 32, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Venereal pleasures; an archaic literary phrase for the pleasures of sexual activity, derived from Venus, the Roman goddess of love.", "english_term": "venereal pleasures", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Multi-word phrase typical of 16th–18th-century Italian prose and poetry; 'venerico' (from Latin venereus, relating to Venus) was the standard adjective for anything pertaining to sexual pleasure; 'malattia venerea' (venereal disease) survives as a set phrase in formal Italian; the phrase sounds archaic-ornate to modern ears and is unlikely to be used sincerely outside historical quotation."}
{"term": "delitto contro il pudore", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "indecency", "concept": "indecent_act", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Crime against decency; the Italian Penal Code category covering sexual offenses against public morality, including exhibitionism, obscene acts, and related violations of 'pudore' (modesty).", "english_term": "crime against decency", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Title IX of Book II of the Codice Rocco (1930) was headed 'Dei delitti contro la moralità pubblica e il buon costume'; 'delitti contro il pudore' formed a specific subcategory protecting 'pudore' as the legal value; Law 66/1996 reclassified sexual offenses as crimes against the person rather than against public morality, effectively retiring this framing and replacing 'pudore' with 'libertà sessuale' as the protected interest."}
{"term": "oscenità", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "obscenity", "concept": "obscenity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 30, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Obscenity; sexually explicit or morally offensive material, speech, or behavior that violates standards of public decency.", "english_term": "obscenity", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Common Italian word still in everyday use; both a live legal term (Arts. 528–529 Codice Penale define 'atti osceni' and 'scritti osceni') and a colloquial intensifier ('ha detto delle oscenità' = he said filthy things); the specifically sexual-legal register feels archaic but the word itself is not dated; no recognition drop expected."}
{"term": "polluzione", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "nocturnal_emission", "concept": "nocturnal_emission", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 20, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "In Catholic moral theology, any sinful sexual emission; specifically nocturnal emission (polluzione notturna) but extended to masturbation and any non-procreative ejaculation outside the conjugal act.", "english_term": "pollution", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From Latin 'pollutio' (ritual defilement); the sexual-theological sense was primary for centuries; in modern Italian 'inquinamento' has displaced it in the environmental sense, so younger speakers encountering 'polluzione' in historical texts may misread it as environmental; 'polluzione notturna' (wet dream) survives as a technical phrase in medical and theological Italian; the moral gravity in Catholic tradition ranked it among the gravest sins against the sixth commandment."}
{"term": "mollezza", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "licentiousness", "concept": "licentiousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 18, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 55, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Softness, moral laxness, and effeminate weakness; in Italian classical and humanist writing, the sexual and moral degeneracy associated with luxury, ease, and unmartial character.", "english_term": "moral softness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Classical Italian moral concept linking sexual passivity and effeminacy to broader political and civic decline; Machiavelli uses 'mollezza' as a vice of decadent rulers contrasted with 'virtù'; the sexual component is always implied — penetrability and passive submission are the physical correlates — but 'mollezza' is never exclusively sexual; implies that luxury saps the masculine energy required for political and military greatness."}
{"term": "effeminatezza", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "effeminacy", "concept": "effeminate_man_slur", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 18, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Effeminacy; the quality of a man displaying feminine mannerisms, appearance, or character, historically carrying implications of sexual passivity, moral weakness, and homosexuality.", "english_term": "effeminacy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard Italian noun for effeminacy; not a slur per se (unlike 'frocio' or 'checca') but functions as an insult in contexts framing effeminacy as sexually or morally shameful; in classical Italian moral thought 'effeminatezza' was simultaneously a sexual failing (the passive role) and a political one (compromising civic and martial virtue); the concept is now partially reclaimed in Italian LGBTQ+ culture."}
{"term": "rammollimento", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "emasculation", "concept": "emasculation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 18, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Softening, moral weakening, or emasculation; the process by which a man becomes effeminate, sexually passive, and morally inert through excess comfort or corrupting influences.", "english_term": "going soft", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literally 'softening' (from 'rammollire,' to soften); in archaic Italian moral-political discourse implied both sexual passivity and civic-military degeneration; 'rammollimento cerebrale' (cerebral softening, i.e., late-stage syphilis or dementia) is now more familiar than the moral-sexual sense; the word sounds clinical or unintentionally comic to modern speakers when used in a sexual-moral register."}
{"term": "impudicizia", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "unchastity", "concept": "licentiousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 22, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Immodesty, unchastity, and sexual licentiousness; the quality of being sexually immodest or morally unrestrained; violation of the virtue of pudicizia (chastity).", "english_term": "unchastity", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From Latin 'impudicitia'; a standard term in Catholic moral theology for sexual immorality short of the gravest offenses; specifically the vice opposed to the cardinal feminine virtue of 'pudicizia'; used in confessional manuals, moral theology textbooks, and ecclesiastical legal proceedings; rarely heard in secular modern Italian outside historical or theological contexts."}
{"term": "impudico", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "wanton_behavior", "concept": "wanton_behavior", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 28, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Immodest, unchaste, and sexually licentious; as noun, a person who violates sexual and moral decency norms; lewd and shameless in conduct.", "english_term": "lewd", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Adjective and noun from Latin 'impudicus'; closer to 'unchaste' or 'immodest' than to the cruder English 'lewd'; describes a character trait rather than a specific act; notably in Roman usage 'impudicus' specifically meant the passive partner in male-male sex, carrying particular shame; this anatomical specificity was lost as the word generalized across centuries of Italian usage."}
{"term": "corruzione", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "debauchery", "concept": "debauchery", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 18, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Moral corruption; in sexual contexts, the seduction or debasement of a person's morals through sexual vice; also a specific Penal Code offense involving the sexual corruption of minors.", "english_term": "corruption", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "'Corruzione di minorenne' (corruption of a minor) remains a specific offense under Italian law; the primary modern meaning is bribery (Arts. 318–319 Codice Penale), making the sexual-moral sense a secondary archaic register; in literary usage implies active debasement of another's character through sexual influence rather than one's own self-indulgence, hence the distinction from 'libertinaggio.'"}
{"term": "seduzione", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "seduction", "concept": "debaucher", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Seduction; the act of persuading or enticing someone to engage in sexual activity, especially outside marriage; historically a criminal offense when targeting a young person with false promises of marriage.", "english_term": "seduction", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "'Seduzione con promessa di matrimonio' (seduction with promise of marriage) was a crime under Art. 526 of the Codice Rocco, repealed 1981; very common word still active across romantic, advertising, and culinary contexts ('seduzione gastronomica'); the sexual-legal sense is archaic but the word is not; mapped to the 'debaucher' concept because seduction is precisely the act of leading another into licentiousness, even though 'seduzione' is an act noun rather than an agent noun."}
{"term": "postribolo", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "brothel", "concept": "brothel", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 35, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Brothel; an establishment where sexual services are sold; an archaic and literary synonym for 'bordello' or 'casa di tolleranza.'", "english_term": "brothel", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From Latin 'prostibulum'; used in literary, historical, and journalistic contexts; more elevated register than 'bordello' (common) or 'casino' (crude slang); the Merlin Law (Law 75/1958) closed Italy's state-licensed brothels ('case chiuse'), after which 'postribolo' became entirely historical in application; the word still appears in literary and political rhetoric as a metaphor for moral corruption."}
{"term": "gigolò", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "male_sex_worker", "concept": "gigolo", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 30, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A man financially kept by a wealthier woman, typically an older one, in exchange for companionship and sexual access; a male escort or sex worker who services female clients.", "english_term": "gigolo", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct French loanword (from 'gigolette,' a female dancehall girl); fully naturalized in Italian; invariable in the plural (i gigolò); carries connotations of elegant parasitism and studied charm rather than street-level prostitution; the female counterpart in Italian is 'mantenuta' (kept woman), though the asymmetric social judgment between the two terms is considerable."}
{"term": "incontro galante", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "sexual_assignation", "concept": "intercourse_implied", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 40, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A gallant encounter; an archaic euphemism for a romantic or sexual assignation, implying elegance and discretion around an illicit or prearranged amorous meeting.", "english_term": "gallant encounter", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "'Galante' in 18th-century Italian usage implied refined courtship and amorous pursuit; 'avventura galante,' 'conversazione galante,' and 'incontro galante' were synonymous circumlocutions for affairs or sexual meetings in aristocratic culture; the word set is now heavily dated and reads as comic if used sincerely in modern Italian; 'galante' itself has narrowed to mean merely 'chivalrous' in contemporary usage, losing most of its erotic charge."}
{"term": "svago carnale", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "sensuality", "concept": "carnal_desire", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 12, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 28, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Carnal diversion; an archaic euphemistic phrase for sexual activity framed as a bodily recreation or pastime of the flesh.", "english_term": "carnal pastime", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "'Svago' (diversion, recreation, hobby) paired with 'carnale' (of the flesh) produces a distancing euphemism that frames sex as a trivial bodily amusement; typical of 18th-century and early 19th-century prose registers; sounds comic or deliberately ironic to modern Italian speakers; very low current recognition among speakers under 50, primarily encountered in historical fiction or period drama subtitles."}
{"term": "rendez-vous", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "sexual_assignation", "concept": "intercourse_implied", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "loanword", "crude": 8, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A prearranged romantic or sexual meeting, especially a clandestine one; French loanword used in Italian to imply discretion and sophistication around an amorous appointment.", "english_term": "rendezvous", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "French loanword used identically in Italian and English; always written with hyphen in formal Italian; French pronunciation preserved; implies secrecy and elegance absent from the plain Italian 'appuntamento'; sexual implication is contextual rather than inherent; increasingly displaced by 'appuntamento' or the English loanword 'date' in modern Italian, especially among younger speakers."}
{"term": "cicisbeo", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "institutionalized_companion", "concept": "cicisbeo_companion", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 28, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "In 18th-century Italian aristocratic society, a married woman's publicly acknowledged male companion and escort — not a secret lover but an institutionalized courtly attendant who accompanied her socially and typically had an implied or openly tolerated sexual relationship with her, with the husband's tacit or explicit approval.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Uniquely Italian 18th-century institution with no direct English equivalent; the cicisbeo (also called 'cavalier servente') was socially normalized in Piedmontese, Venetian, and Neapolitan aristocratic culture; crucially distinguished from a secret lover or gigolo by being publicly acknowledged and institutionally sanctioned, often with the husband's formal consent documented in marriage contracts; etymology disputed (possibly from 'cici,' trinket, or onomatopoeia for whispering flirtation); declined with Napoleonic reforms and the Risorgimento's bourgeois moral reconfiguration."}
{"term": "mantenuta", "lang": "it", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "kept_woman", "concept": "kept_woman", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 30, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A kept woman; a woman financially maintained by a wealthy man, typically outside of marriage, in exchange for sexual availability and exclusive companionship.", "english_term": "kept woman", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Feminine past participle of 'mantenere' (to maintain/support) used as a standalone noun; distinguished from a prostitute by exclusivity and ongoing financial arrangement rather than per-act payment; the male equivalent 'mantenuto' (kept man) carries substantially stronger social stigma; the word still circulates in contemporary Italian journalism and tabloid culture; its female counterpart for the male role is 'gigolò,' itself a French loanword."}
